>BYE BYE TOURIST.
>
>I hadn't saved him, so my near sure fire ascencion was cut a little short.
>
>Moral of this story:  NEVER, NEVER, NEVER put a storage device at Q.

I use the #adjust command all the time.  I tend to use:
a-Best weapon
b-Blindfold
c-camera (for tourists)
d-holy water (just cause I got used to it being there when I played Priests)
h-bags of holding (or whatever bag I'm currently using)
k-key (or lock-pick, or whatever)
p-pick axe
u-unicorn horn
w-magic whistle (or whatever whistle)

This can lead to it's own problems.  I get so used to the things being
in those spots that I make mistakes when they're not.  Particularly 
tourists.  I tend to die a lot at the start when I play a tourist.
After a given restart, I hit type "ac4" to zap the floating eye
to my left with my camera.  Since I've forgotten to move the camera
to c, ac produces an error, and 4 walks me right into it.  I can't
tell you how many times I've done that.

If I ever get around to it, I'd like change the code to let you 
specify this in your configuration file.  I'm not quite sure 
what the interface would be, particularly for complex letters
like k (which I want to hold the best key-type device I have).

Of course, I'd also like to change the inventory interface so
that armor doesn't appear in your pack if you're wearing it.  
That might be an even tougher change to make, but it could free
up a whole bunch of inventory letters.  It'd work like this:
armor you're wearing doesn't get an inverntory letter.  If you
want to take it off, you just type T and then one of bhfsg (for 
Body, Head, Foot, Sheild, Gloves).  Other things like dipping
you'd probably have to hit a special key (maybe "[" ie: "#d[g" to
#dip your gloves).

This of course leads to the next change I've wanted to make for 
a long time: Merging the W key and the P key to do the same 
thing (make it easier to wear a towel ("ab"-nope "Wb"-nope "Pb").
Of course I also wanted to Merge the T and R keys to do the same
thing, but I may have to rethink my interface.  Maybe T (or R)
should present you with a question "what do you want to remove:
[bfghlrs [=?]"  You hit ? and you get:
  Body armor:   elven cloak
  Footwear:     speed boots
  Gloves:       gauntlets of power
  Head gear:    Helm of opposite alignment
  Left finger:  ring of polymorph
  Right finger: ring of polymorph control
  Sheild:       Sheild of reflection
  [:            all armor
  = :           all rings

Then we might get a couple of rings out of your inventory too.
Body armor is (of course) only your outermost piece.

Of course, when doing this I'd also want to think about allowing
users to easily rebind their keys.  The user should be able to 
specify in the configuration file a list of commands and keys. 
The default would be:
a:apply
c:close
d:drop
...
R:Remove
T:Take_off

If I introduced a new command called Takeoff(eh?), the user could
then change R and T by specifying in their config file:
R:Takeoff(eh?)
T:Takeoff(eh?)

Just to make this even more complicated, I'd also add a new
command like the # command which would allow you to type out
the long version of any command (in case you don't have a given
command mapped to anything).  Even these keys could be specified
in your config file.
#:Extended_command
*:Long_extended_command (you have to type the whole thing out)

So to get rid of the "accientally hitting Qy" problem, you could
specify:
q:quaff
Q:quaff
The only way to quit would be to type *quity.
 
Of course, while I am a nethack addict, being married and having
a full time job makes it unlikely that I'll actually get around 
to doing all this anytime soon.  I guess I'll just leave it on
my 3.2 wish list.
